  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1025 AM MDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nMOSTLY DRY AND SUNNY TO END THE WORK WEEK:\n\nHigh pressure continues to dominate with the center of the system to \nour southwest. This will allow flow aloft to remain zonal, \nkeeping conditions mostly dry and sunny. Because of this, \ndaytime highs will run 5-10 degrees above normal through the end\nof the work week. As a result of ample daytime heating, upper \nlevel winds will begin to mix down towards the surface today and\nFriday. Afternoon gusts of 20-30 mph are expected in addition \nto daytime minimum humidities below 15%. Hence, localized \ncritical fire weather conditions will be in place today and \nFriday, particularly in southwest Colorado. \n\nMOISTURE PUSH THIS WEEKEND:\n\nModels continue to bring in moisture for the weekend, although it \nstill remains unclear as to the specific timing and intensity of \nshowers and storms. For now, it appears that Sunday afternoon holds \nthe best chance for precipitation, but chances don't look very \nwidespread. As the weekend progresses, a low pressure system will \nswing into the PacNW Saturday, tracking across the Northern Rockies \ninto Sunday. This will shift the dominant flow aloft to \nsouthwesterly, a favorable flow for moisture advection. Although one \ncaveat presents itself...a lack of synoptic forcing. All this \nmoisture is no good if there's nothing to force showers and storms \nto form. So besides favorable southwesterly orographics, a weak \nfrontal boundary may work to enhance shower and storm activity, but \neven that appears to diffuse as it reaches the area later this \nweekend. Keep an eye on the forecast as changes are possible as more \nguidance becomes available closer to the weekend.  \n\nAside from the moisture push, this weekend will see more in the way \nof cloud cover, which will promote cooler temperatures throughout \nthe area. Additionally, breezy afternoons will be favored with this \nset up. Gusts in the 25-35 mph range are expected and daytime \nminimum humidities will remain above 15%.",
